## How I would verify it

All results below are **expected**, not observed.

| Browser check | Expected result |
|---|---|
| Open the page with DevTools Console and Network visible. | The desert, road, car, and HUD render. The module loads without errors. |
| Wait at the starting line, then accelerate. Try both key sets. | The timer stays at zero until the car moves. Both key sets work, and braking slows the car to a stop. |
| Steer onto the shoulder and keep accelerating. | The car slows on gravel. Steering farther out cannot take it past the shoulder or let it cut across the infield. |
| Hold accelerate without steering until the car crosses the checkerboard. | Nothing counts at the initial line position. The first full circuit sets a best time and starts the next lap. |
| Brake for several seconds during the next lap, then finish it. Reload the page. | The slower lap does not replace the best. The best survives reload if browser storage is available. |
| Press `R` mid-lap. Switch tabs while holding accelerate, release it, and return. | Restart clears the current run without clearing the best. Returning to the tab does not leave acceleration stuck. |
| Resize to 320 px, test at 200% zoom, and emulate reduced motion. | The HUD remains readable. Reduced motion replaces the chase camera with a fixed overhead view. |
| Block the CDN request and reload. | The HUD shows a load error instead of staying on “Loading track…”. |

The current timer uses simulation time, so it does not charge the player for time spent in a hidden tab. The fixed overhead view removes camera travel while keeping the same driving controls.

Save this as `index.html` to play **Mint Circuit**, a one-track Three.js time trial. Use the arrow keys or WASD to drive, and press R to restart without clearing your best lap. The game uses one file, with no build step or art assets. It loads a pinned version of Three.js from a CDN, so the browser needs internet access.

**Model the domain.** The track is a closed centerline, the car has a signed distance along it and a sideways offset, and the timer records the next finish line the car must reach. I chose track-relative driving over free steering with checkpoints. The tradeoff is that the car follows the bends automatically, but cutting across the infield cannot count as a lap.

**Throughput checkpoint**
- **Blocking first steps.** Choose the track coordinates and finish-line rule before writing the scene, controls, or HUD.
- **Independent workstreams.** The scene and HUD are separate sections of the same file. No parallel work was needed.
- **Shared mutable state.** The driving update owns race state. The camera and HUD read it each frame.
- **Smallest safe decomposition.** One file and one owner. This chat has no delegation or browser tools, so runtime verification remains open.

